 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why a hypoglossal palsy makes the tongue point at the lesion",
   "html": "<p>The genioglossus protrudes the tongue by pulling its root forward, and each side pushes the tongue toward the opposite side. Lose one hypoglossal nerve and the healthy genioglossus is unopposed, so on protrusion the tongue deviates toward the weak side. It is a quick bedside test that follows directly from the muscle's action.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's extrinsic muscle table and its protrusion line.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why the tongue needs four cranial nerves",
   "html": "<p>The odd nerve supply is embryology showing through. The anterior two thirds come from the first pharyngeal arch, so general sensation follows V3, while taste is carried by CN VII, whose chorda tympani hitches a ride on the lingual nerve. The posterior third comes from the third arch and takes CN IX for both, and the base from the fourth arch takes CN X. The muscles come from occipital somites, which is why they follow CN XII.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's sensory and motor supply slides, read with the three-part division of the tongue.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why stones form in the submandibular gland",
   "html": "<p>Wharton's duct runs uphill: it travels forward and upward above the mylohyoid to open at the sublingual papilla, so drainage works against gravity. The submandibular saliva is also more viscous and richer in calcium than the parotid's. Those two facts together are why the great majority of salivary stones form here, classically giving pain and swelling under the jaw at mealtimes.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's submandibular duct description and its mixed gland composition.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why mumps hurts to chew",
   "html": "<p>The parotid is wrapped in a tough fascial capsule and sits between the mandible and the mastoid. When it swells, the capsule will not stretch, so pressure rises inside it, and moving the mandible presses on the gland further. The facial nerve running through the gland also explains why parotid surgery risks facial palsy, and why an enlarging parotid mass that causes facial weakness is a worrying sign.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's parotid gland slide, including the facial nerve passing through it.</p>"
  }
 ],
